  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">GenDev: Engendering Industries</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(USAID Bureau for Inclusive Growth, Partnerships, and Innovation (IPI)):  The objective of this contract is to continue scaling up USAID&apos;s Engendering Industries program to increase gender equality and women&apos;s economic security within traditionally male-dominated industries. Engendering Industries will include a focus on partnering with local organizations and engaging with USAID Missions interested in buying into the contract, in addition to gender equality and economic growth impacts. With FY24 funding, Engendering Industries will continue expanding the number of companies, partner types, sectors, and countries in order to maximize impact around the world. Under this Engendering Industries Task Order new sector priorities include, but are not limited to: Transportation, Digital, Infrastructure, Waste Management and Recycling, Finance, Agro-Processing, Water, Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH), Construction, Urban Planning Commissions, as well as industries prioritized by Prosper Africa, such as Cocoa, Shea Butter, and Coffee. Through customized best practices, demand-driven coaching, and specialized training programs, Engendering Industries will continue to build the capacity of organizational leaders to implement change management interventions that increase gender equality and improve business performance.  ..Successful implementation of activities combined with a robust communications and thought leadership strategy has successfully positioned the Engendering Industries approach as the gold standard for advancing gender equality in lucrative, male-dominated industries worldwide.  During its most recent five-year contract, Engendering Industries partnered with 68 organizations in 39 countries, including 47 organizations across 30 countries in its Intensive Program and 21 organizations across 15 countries through its Accelerated Program.  Engendering Industries will further efforts to institutionalize GBV prevention and response in the workforce as a key strategy in a</narrative>
  </description>
